identifier: CHEBI:33658
preferred_term: Aromatic hydrocarbon
ontology_mapping:
  ontology_id: CHEBI:33658
  ontology_label: arene
  ontology_source: CHEBI
  mapping_quality: SYNONYM_MATCH
  evidence:
  - evidence_type: LEXICAL_MATCH
    source: kgm-metatraits
    notes: 'Imported from kgm-metatraits via source-preset. source_id=kgm.metatraits.special:aromatic_hydrocarbon; '
  - evidence_type: DATABASE_MATCH
    source: MIM curation (#322)
    notes: 'mapping_quality EXACT_MATCH -> BROAD_MATCH. CHEBI:33848 carries no structural
      definition, so it is a class rather than an individual, and the record''s label
      ''Aromatic hydrocarbon'' does not equal the term label ''polycyclic arene''.
      EXACT_MATCH therefore asserted an identity the pair does not have. Re-graded
      because the record is BROADER than the term: benzene is an aromatic hydrocarbon
      and is not a polycyclic arene, so narrowMatch would be backwards.'
  - evidence_type: MANUAL_CURATION
    source: MIM curation (SSSOM delivery review)
    notes: 'Regrounded CHEBI:33848 (kgmicrobe.compound:aromatic_hydrocarbon) -> CHEBI:33658
      ''arene''. CHEBI:33658 is defined as ''Any monocyclic or polycyclic aromatic
      hydrocarbon'', which is exactly what this record names; CHEBI:33848 ''polycyclic
      arene'' is a proper subset of it. The old shape asserted skos:broadMatch — the
      corpus''s only one — and kg-microbe''s consolidator reads narrowMatch and broadMatch
      identically as ''the MIM subject is NARROWER than the object'', so it published
      this record as a CHILD of polycyclic arene: backwards, and chemically false.
      The record''s own history carried both readings, the regrade calling it broader
      and the mint that followed calling it narrower. Grounding at the term that actually
      denotes it makes the mapping symmetric and true, which removes the disagreement
      rather than arguing about direction, and retires a registry mint the record
      never needed — a mint gives identity to a record NARROWER than every available
      term. Not a #322 class overstatement: both sides are the same class.'
synonyms: []
mapping_status: MAPPED
